\details{
In the printed "Group differences (main effects)" block, a
threshold difference that rests on the prior rather than on the data is
marked with a leading \code{*}, and the block gains the footnote \emph{a group lacks
observations in this category or in the reference category; the estimate
reflects the prior, not the data}.

\code{bgmCompare()} keeps the union of the categories the groups observe, so a
group can contribute no observations at all to a retained category. Two
things follow. If the empty category is the row's own, that group's
threshold for it has nothing behind it. If the empty category is the
reference category, the group has no data fixing the level of its
threshold vector at all, and \emph{every} threshold of that variable is
affected, not just one --- so the whole variable is marked. Either way the
reported difference is large and very uncertain without being evidence of
a group difference.

The per-group counts behind the mark are in
\code{extract_arguments(fit)$category_support}, one matrix of category-by-group
observation counts per variable (\code{NULL} for Blume-Capel variables, which
are exempt from the union recode and are never marked). Fits made before
that field existed print unmarked.

The inclusion table (\verb{$indicator}) reports, per difference indicator, the Rao-Blackwellized
inclusion probability with its Monte Carlo standard error, effective sample
size and split-R-hat. At every update of a difference indicator the sampler
proposes to flip it and records the probability that the difference is in the
model after the move: the acceptance probability \eqn{\alpha} of adding it
when it is out, \eqn{1 - \alpha}, the probability of retaining it, when it
is in. The
inclusion probability is the average of those recorded values, and \code{mcse},
\code{n_eff} and \code{Rhat} are computed from their series.

\code{mcse} and \code{n_eff} are \code{NA}, and print blank, when the series has no
variation the Monte Carlo machinery can work on; \code{Rhat} is \code{NA} when every
chain is exactly constant, and otherwise computed. For a difference the data
support overwhelmingly, the probability of accepting its removal is below
about \code{1e-16} at every update, so \eqn{1 - \alpha} rounds to exactly 1 in
double precision and the series is constant at 1 in every draw of every
chain; a difference the data rule out as strongly gives a series constant at 0. A
series that varies by less than about \code{3e-8} (a variance below \code{1e-15}) is
below the floor at which the ESS kernel stops estimating and is treated the
same way. In both cases the inclusion probability stayed within \code{3e-8} of 1 or 0
throughout the run, so there is no Monte Carlo error at any printed digit
and the verdict lies far beyond any evidence threshold; a difference whose series
varies by more than that shows numbers. A series constant at a value away
from 0 and 1 would give the same blanks, but only a sampler that never moved
the indicator in any chain produces it.
Main-effect difference rows are \code{NA} throughout when
\code{main_difference_selection = FALSE}: their indicators were never sampled.
